// system/darwin — the Darwin calls and layouts the standard library needs.
//
// Split from `system/posix` because POSIX does not define these: `struct stat`'s
// layout is the system's own, and `_NSGetExecutablePath` is Darwin's answer to
// `/proc/self/exe`. Sibling of `system/linux` and `system/windows`.

module "system/darwin"

/// `_NSGetExecutablePath` (`<mach-o/dyld.h>`). 0 on success, non-zero when
/// `buf` was too small.
///
/// ⚠️ C writes the NEEDED capacity back through `size` and Vader never sees it:
/// a scalar `@c_pointer` cannot be an out-parameter — the `!` that would ask for
/// one is `T3075`, and the shim's temporary dies at the return. A caller wanting
/// to retry at the right size must lend a one-element array instead. The one
/// caller does not: it starts at 4096, four times `PATH_MAX` on Darwin, and
/// treats failure as "give up".
///
/// Darwin's answer to `/proc/self/exe`. The path it gives may be relative or
/// carry symlinks, so callers pass it through `realpath`.
@c_header("<mach-o/dyld.h>")
@extern("_NSGetExecutablePath")
export ns_get_executable_path :: fn(buf: u8[]!, @c_pointer size: u32) -> i32
